  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research the serial frequency-counting kernel. Replace unordered_map allocation, clearing and repeated lookups with a reusable open-addressed table containing full signed keys and multiplicities. Size capacity for at most scan_length+1 distinct prefixes at load factor no greater than one half; compare generation tags with touched-slot resetting. Use portable unsigned hash arithmetic and explicit key comparisons, not a sentinel that excludes a valid prefix. Seed frequency[0]=1, query prefix-k before inserting prefix, and preserve repeated-prefix behavior when k=0. Keep 64-bit answer accumulation. Initially isolate these changes from threading and traversal changes so their measured contribution is identifiable.",
        "B": "Research parallel execution on at most four CPU workers, using threading supported by the unchanged build. Boundary pairs are independent: compare flattened triangular-pair scheduling with load-balanced chunks of outer boundaries, avoiding the imbalance of equal contiguous outer-loop partitions. Keep frequency tables, scratch storage and answer accumulators private to workers; reduce answers once rather than using hot-loop atomics. Reuse workers across the entire computation. Measure serial, two-worker and four-worker variants and consider a workload-based serial crossover because the baseline is subsecond. Retain exact signed-input semantics and coordinate with G on how scheduling interacts with C's incremental versus independent-band layouts.",
        "C": "Research dimension choice, contiguous data access and input overhead independently of specialized hashing or threading. Normalize the matrix to S=min(M,N) paired rows and L=max(M,N) contiguous scan positions, giving O(S^2 L) work for every aspect ratio. Compare incrementally accumulated bands with boundary-prefix arrays laid out as prefix[boundary][position], so each band reads contiguous arrays instead of striding through the current row-prefix matrix. Compare fused compression/counting with a separate vectorizable compression pass where useful. Evaluate unsynchronized, untied C++ input against a correctly signed buffered parser; remove redundant initialization only when every subsequently read element is initialized. Measure these changes separately where practical. Do not assume square matrices, nonnegative values or a particular input distribution.",
        "G": "Integrate only programs and improvements produced by the research trees. Preserve the strongest measured standalone candidate and compare useful combinations of A's frequency table, B's scheduling and C's orientation/layout; do not assume their gains add. In particular, compare independent boundary-prefix tasks against incremental-band reuse under parallel scheduling. Avoid duplicate transposes, redundant preprocessing and shared mutable frequency storage. Use the unchanged automatic correctness and timing process, distinguish repeatable gains from baseline variation, and retain the baseline if no candidate improves it. Audit one-dimensional matrices, mixed signs, negative targets, repeated prefixes, k=0 and 64-bit count limits. Keep the compiler, tests, oracle, budgets, target and permissions unchanged, and output only the required integer and newline.",
        "analysis": "All roles optimize the supplied Problem A program, not the other problems in the document. The only measured candidate is the correct baseline: mean 0.266127 seconds, samples 0.258001–0.273783 seconds, with four correctness cases. These observations do not reveal input shapes or sign distributions. Source inspection suggests three complementary opportunities: allocation-heavy hash counting, independent boundary-pair parallelism, and dimension/layout improvements over O(M*N^2). The target is four ARM64 CPU cores with no GPU. Every valid signed input must remain correct; the maximum answer is 249500250000, requiring 64-bit accumulation. Bottleneck and speedup claims remain hypotheses until measured."
      },
