    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Refine the scalar winner 718780280eb84e1e. Retain smaller-dimension boundary pairing, contiguous scans and generation-tagged open addressing. Compare its 64-bit keys with compact signed 32-bit keys while retaining unsigned hash arithmetic, explicit key equality, safe generation reset and capacity >= 2*(scan_length+1). Prefix multiplicities fit 32 bits; the answer must remain 64-bit. Investigate a combined consume operation that overlaps independent query/insertion probes without mutating the table before reading the query result. Measure each change separately; keep this lane scalar and single-threaded.",
        "B": "Investigate ARM64 SIMD and removal of the running-prefix dependency, starting from A's winning orientation rather than the slower original layout. Build boundary-major two-dimensional prefixes so each band's prefix values are independent differences. First compare this representation using the same scalar counter; then evaluate four-lane NEON probing and unsigned hash preparation separately. Compare materialized preparation against a fused or short-block path to expose scratch-array overhead. Keep frequency updates sequential and query before incrementing, including for k=0. INT_MIN is a valid empty-key sentinel under the proven bounds. Treat sentinel clearing and generation tagging as alternatives, not cumulative optimizations.",
        "C": "Apply parallelism to A's fast contiguous-layout/flat-table kernel, not the legacy unordered_map kernel. Partition the smaller dimension's triangular interval space across at most four cores. Compare equal contiguous pair ranges decoded once per worker against coarse dynamically scheduled batches. Allocate one reusable counter per worker, share only completed read-only prefixes, and combine answers through a 64-bit reduction. Avoid per-pair allocation, shared hash updates and unnecessary parallel-region launches. Reassess the serial crossover using the faster kernel; do not inherit the old 32768/65536 thresholds without measurement. Preserve a serial fallback without changing compiler settings.",
        "G": "Retain 718780280eb84e1e as the incumbent and fallback. Prioritize a measured integration of its layout/counter with C's private-table parallelism, then evaluate A's scalar refinements and B's representation/probing alternatives individually. Choose one coherent prefix representation and counter implementation; do not blindly merge whole programs. Measure pairwise combinations before attempting a larger combination, and retain only combinations that actually outperform the incumbent through the unchanged evaluation pipeline. Independently assess source-only fast iostream initialization if useful. Audit negative keys, repeated prefixes, k=0, extreme sums, rectangular orientation and 64-bit totals. Preserve stdin/stdout behavior and all valid-input semantics; change no tests, oracle, compiler, target or budgets.",
        "analysis": "Optimize only the supplied Problem A submatrix counter. The baseline is 0.266127 s; A's smaller-dimension, contiguous-prefix, flat-table program leads at 0.046328 s, about 5.74x faster. B's NEON/table program reaches 0.079442 s; C's dynamic/static parallel programs reach 0.092763/0.100400 s; two-dimensional prefixes alone reach 0.247069 s. These bundled measurements do not isolate causes or establish additive gains. Prioritize the winning scalar foundation, then test SIMD and four-core parallelism against it. For all valid inputs, |prefix| <= 998001000 and |prefix-k| <= 1098001000, permitting signed 32-bit keys, but the maximum rectangle count is 249500250000 and requires 64-bit accumulation."
      },
